schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Infrastructure Follows Function
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Before choosing a knowledge-management tool, ask what work this part of
    the commons needs to do (explain a concept, inventory resources, track
    decisions, publish a guide) and pick the substrate that fits that job —
    not the tool with the nicest landing page. Corollary: no single tool
    needs to do everything; a healthy commons is usually a hybrid.
  source_traditions:
    - >-
      Regen Knowledge Commons Toolkit, Layer 10 (Infrastructure & Substrate)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used as the article's governing principle for its tool-by-job directory,
    and again for its public/internal/private visibility-tier mapping (each
    tier gets its own substrate rather than one tool for everything).
  adjacent_meanings:
    - >-
      the portability test — "would your knowledge survive the tool dying?"
      (plain-text/Git as the durable baseline, everything else as a
      publishing or collaboration layer on top)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      A tool can make a workflow easier, but it can also distort the commons
      around its own assumptions — infrastructure should serve the work, not
      the reverse.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Picking a tool for its polish or landing page rather than the specific
    job (explaining, inventorying, tracking, publishing) it needs to do —
    the article names this as the most common way commons rot.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/knowledge-tools-directory.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
